WEBVTT

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.560
Harper's Weekly, 1883.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.220
It so happens that the work which is likely to be our most durable monument, and to convey

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:16.850
some knowledge of us to the most remote posterity, is a work of bare utility.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.960
Not a shrine, not a fortress, not a palace, but a bridge.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.590
It's the spirit behind the Brooklyn Bridge, and the spirit which is in the structure itself,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:54.270
the spirit that you anticipate and appreciate, even if you've never studied engineering,

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:00.000
even if you've never studied aesthetics, it's there, it's there, and in one degree

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.800
or another people who use it and have their eyes open respond to it when Neil

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.440
Armstrong landed on the moon there was an interview published in the New York

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.770
one of the New York papers with a woman on Long Island and they asked her what

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:22.590
her response to all this excitement was and she said it was very interesting but

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.830
it was nothing compared to the day they opened the Brooklyn Bridge she had been

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.330
there

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.820
It was the biggest bridge in the world.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.100
Everyone in Brooklyn and New York agreed.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.160
It had absolutely no rivals.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.820
The bridge was the vision of John A. Roebling,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:26.650
an immigrant, a genius, a civil engineer.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.000
It was finally completed under the most difficult circumstances

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.610
imaginable by his son, Washington Roebling. The bridge was half again as big as any built before

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:42.300
it. Its two stone towers were taller than any structure on either shore, taller than anything

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:48.250
else on the entire North American continent. The roadway of the bridge was as spacious as Fifth

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:54.090
Avenue, and down the center of the roadway, above the traffic, was an elevated promenade where

00:03:54.090 --> 00:04:00.000
people could go to enjoy the views of the bay and of the cities as never before. And the distinctive

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.940
of cables that suspended the bridge was made of steel, the new metal of a new age.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:30.000
It took 14 long years to build the bridge from 1869 to 1883, years during which Edison

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.870
Wilson invented the lightbulb and the phonograph.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:37.460
Alexander Graham Bell the telephone.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.080
The buffalo were exterminated on the Great Plains.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.420
The curveball and catcher's mask were introduced into baseball.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:49.910
President Chester A. Arthur installed plumbing in the White House.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:05:00.000
In Europe, Marx died, and Einstein, Lennon, and Picasso were born.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.500
extraordinary man and if you people in Brooklyn are wise you will interfere

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:10.080
with his views as little as possible give the old man his way and trust him

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:18.630
how and where John Roebling found time to do all he did to attend conventions

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:23.170
and write voluminously for scientific journals practice the flute and the

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:29.480
piano study metaphysics pour forth these lucubrations on thousands of pages of

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:30.000
manuscripts

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.130
invent tools and machinery, design canals and bridges and himself superintend their construction.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:42.090
How he achieved all this, I say, bewilders imagination.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:49.160
The contemplated work, when constructed in accordance with my designs,

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.210
will not only be the greatest bridge in existence.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.290
It will be the greatest engineering feat of the continent and of the age.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.000
As a great work of art and a science,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.520
a successful specimen of advanced bridge engineering.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.680
This structure will forever testify to the energy,

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.400
enterprise, and wealth of that community,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.390
which shall secure its erection.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:17.100
Respectfully submitted, John A. Roebling,

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:25.810
September 1st, 1867.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:27.830
Roebling had grown up in the walled village

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.440
of Mulhausen, Germany.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.100
As the first member of his family ever to leave the village, he attended the Royal Polytechnic

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:39.770
Institute in Berlin, where he studied engineering and philosophy, and was reputedly the favorite

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:43.510
student of the great George Frederick Hegel.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:47.010
According to family tradition, it was Hegel who inspired the young man to go to the New

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:53.550
World, to resolve his destiny in all that space.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:58.590
In the western reaches of Pennsylvania, he founded the village of Saxenburg, a utopian

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.000
agrarian community.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.170
It was there six years later that his first son, Washington, was born.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.880
To me as a child, Saxonburg was the finest place in the world.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:16.510
But my father began to realize he had not been cut out by nature for a farmer.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:17.770
He lacked patience.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:23.030
So he took to engineering again, his true vocation, and let my mother do the farming,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.320
which she did very well when he would let her.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:30.000
From the start, John.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.830
Roebling was fascinated by the suspension bridge form. With the towers in

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:40.210
compression and the cables in tension, he saw the suspension bridge as the

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:47.710
perfect equilibrium of nature. In some foreign scientific journals, Roebling had

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:52.070
read that by twisting a number of iron wires together, one could produce an iron

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.410
rope of great strength and flexibility.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:08:00.000
Having himself seen two men die on a portage canal by the failure of ordinary hemp

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.110
rope, Roebling understood the importance of the invention.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:08.150
In a small shed behind his house, he started the first wire rope factory in America.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.380
Ultimately, it made him rich, and it freed him from the drudgery of the farm.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:29.170
You drive over to Suspension Bridge and divide your misery between the chances of smashing

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:30.000
down.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.560
200 feet into the river below and the chances of having a railway train

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:41.240
overhead smashing down on you. Either possibility is discomforting taken by

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:46.570
itself but mixed together they amount in the aggregate to positive unhappiness.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:54.770
Mark Twain. The most striking features of this iron web were the cables

00:08:54.770 --> 00:09:00.000
radiating from its towers. In an age when one out of every four bridges built was collapsed

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:08.530
these diagonal stays gave a Roebling bridge its strength and beauty. John

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:13.350
Roebling's next bridge was over the Allegheny River at Pittsburgh. There he

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:17.270
was joined by his son Washington who had completed a degree in engineering at

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:21.970
Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute and was to become his father's favorite assistant.

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:30.000
Washington went on to receive his practical training as a bridge builder during the Civil

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.180
war. In urgent need of bridges, the Union Army was impressed with his ability and tolerant

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:42.820
of his inexperience. Three bridges and eight battles later, including Gettysburg, they

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:52.450
promoted him to the rank of colonel. When peace came, Washington Roebling moved directly

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:58.260
to Cincinnati, Ohio, where his father was building the biggest suspension bridge in the world.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:05.700
The size and magnitude of this work far surpasses any expectations I had formed of it.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.740
It is the highest thing in this country.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:15.220
It will take me a week to get used to the proportions of everything around here.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:22.020
Increasingly, it was Washington Roebling who assumed responsibility for the day-to-day construction of the bridge,

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.500
freeing John Roebling to pursue his next great plan.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.000
January 1867.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.980
Brooklyn Eagle. In consequence of the extreme cold weather which we have lately experienced,

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:41.030
New York Harbor has been completely frozen over, so persons and even teams have crossed from the

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:46.530
city to Brooklyn. The newspapers have been filled for weeks with vehement demand for the bridge,

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:50.250
which all agree must be built at once, from New York to Brooklyn.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:00.000
For John Roebling also, the Brooklyn Bridge had become the overriding passage

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.240
of his life. In just three months he produced all the drawings, the cross

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:12.000
sections and location plans, made preliminary surveys, took soundings,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:18.990
estimated cost, and wrote his proposal. In plan everything was done with exacting

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:23.820
precision until the proposed structure conformed with both his mathematical and

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.560
spiritual ideal of perfection.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.600
That April, legislation was passed in Albany, creating the New York and Brooklyn Bridge Company,

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:41.340
and its trustees appointed John Roebling chief engineer.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:46.860
The Common Council appropriated one and a half million dollars to start the work, and

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:54.980
in early June of 1869, construction finally began.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:12:00.000
Brooklyn Eagle, an accident occurred on the 28th of June, while Mr.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.170
Mr. Roebling with his son was engaged in surveying for the bridge.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:08.290
He was standing on a string piece inside the Fulton ferry rack.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:13.200
On seeing a boat approach, Mr. Roebling stepped back onto a beam, but a knotty protuberance

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:20.000
caught one of his feet, and the pressure of the oncoming boat crushed it.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:24.020
My father was a hydropathist and had decided ideas of his own.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.720
He refused to listen to the surgeon I brought, dismissed him, and proceeded to cure himself

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.000
with water until he died 17.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.830
17 days later in the most horrible agonies of Lockjaw.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:41.060
John Roebling had proclaimed that his bridge would be the greatest in existence.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:45.930
All his son had to do now was build it.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:51.470
Here I was, 32 years old, suddenly in charge of the most stupendous engineering structure

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:56.930
of the age, with only preparatory plans, nothing fixed or decided.

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.590
The prop on which I hitherto leaned had fallen.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:07.140
Henceforth, I must rely on myself, Washington Roebling.

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:19.510
Caissons, 1869 to 1872, April 9, 1870.

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:21.650
For those not acquainted with the process,

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:25.630
we may say that a caisson is an inverted wooden box

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:28.530
on which the towers are constructed.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.000
As it rests on the river,

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.330
bed. A hollow space called air chamber is formed beneath it. Compressed air is forced into the

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:43.520
chamber until the water is entirely displaced. The chamber is entered by laborers who undermine

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:49.890
the edges of the caisson while masonry is built on top of it, causing in this way a steady and

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:54.570
gradual descent through the riverbed until it's grounded on bedrock.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.640
In theory, it worked.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.980
Small caissons holding one or two men

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:05.650
had been used in Europe.

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:07.030
But the nature of the difficulties

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:10.190
in sinking a caisson half the size of a city block

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:12.390
could hardly be foreseen.

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:15.130
Uncertain that caissons of such extraordinary size

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:16.590
could even be built,

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:18.900
the shipbuilders who were to construct them

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:21.240
refused to guarantee their safe delivery

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:24.380
and demanded all the money in advance.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:25.860
The first caisson would be built

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.450
for the Brooklyn side of the river.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:30.000
- 70,000 tons

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.740
stone and brickwork are to be piled upon the caisson which now floats lightly on

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:40.200
the surface of the East River all the weight of every man woman and child in

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:44.160
this city would be but a fraction of the weight of building materials this

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:52.860
wooden caisson will have to bear few people in New York at that time realized

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:57.610
how complex the caissons actually were that Washington Roebling designed every

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.000
feature he devised an airlock

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:05.020
for admitting the workman, and an ingenious method of removing the earth and rock without

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.660
allowing the compressed air inside to escape.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:12.270
A shaft was passed through the roof of the caisson.

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:17.030
It was filled with a column of water, the water held precariously in place by the force

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:20.460
of the air inside.

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:24.740
Through it a giant clamshell dredge could move freely as it carried the debris up and

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:27.140
out of the caisson to waiting barges.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.150
Each decision was described in a drawing,

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:36.800
hand-painted with meticulous care, often by Roebling himself,

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:41.400
for the benefit of those craftsmen who couldn't read or speak English.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.070
On paper, it all worked perfectly.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.310
June, 1870.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.550
The bed is so rocky that in place of one month,

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:52.610
five are required to do the work.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:56.020
And these are five months of incessant worry and toil,

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:00.000
everlasting breaking down and repairing, and constant study.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:07.540
where to improve if possible. For Roebling and the men working inside the caisson, with all that

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:13.240
weight bearing down overhead and the river outside, it was a terrifying and eerie experience.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:21.470
The force of the compressed air made pulses race, made voices thin and unnatural, and made the danger

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:28.850
of fire very serious. Flaming calcium lamps lit one chamber while the rest of the caisson lay dark

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:30.000
and slimy with river.

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:37.080
mud. Often explosions of compressed air escaping under the edges clung the men

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:42.400
into total darkness as several feet of water rushed in. It was said that

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:50.120
Washington Roebling was the only one who kept his head. June 15, 1870. Rate of

00:16:50.120 --> 00:17:00.000
descent not half a foot this week. The caisson of the East River Bridge was severely damaged by fire

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.530
yesterday. I don't believe any man now living will cross that bridge. I am the

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:10.510
chief engineer and I'm in constant attendance day and night except time for

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:15.150
sleep. A fire broke out Thursday evening and I stayed down until about 5 o'clock.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:20.060
I felt I should be paralyzed if I stayed down another minute. I was partially

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:26.240
paralyzed and I'm not quite over it now. The damage caused by the fire was

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:30.000
repaired at huge cost and effort. The Brooklyn caisson was

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.890
completed on March 11, 1871, and filled with concrete.

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:41.480
Its final position was 44 feet, six inches below water level.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:43.660
The second caisson, now under construction

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.020
for the New York side, would have to sink

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.810
almost 60 feet deeper.

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:52.450
It was being lined with boiler plate to prevent fire,

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:55.680
but the increased air pressure required inside

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:57.590
would cause even graver problems.

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:06.300
The journal of Dr. Andrew H. Smith, surgeon to The Bridge Company.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:12.830
The pathological effects of high atmospheric pressure I propose to call the caisson disease.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:18.390
From the 25th of January, when I assumed medical charge of the men, 110 cases of sickness

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:23.560
occurred which were fairly due to compressed air.

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:30.000
Hugh Rourke, March 12th, had suffered previously from severe pains in the chest and limbs which

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:36.870
him to give up work. Sometime afterward, when the pressure was four atmospheres, he resumed work for

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:43.080
a single day, the labor being excessively severe. He suffered no inconvenience apparently, however,

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:49.900
and after leaving the caisson, washed his hands and face as usual. A moment after he fell senseless, and

00:18:49.900 --> 00:19:00.000
in 15 minutes he was dead. As the caisson descended deeper into the riverbed, the threat of the disease

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:08.270
increased. When the test borings on the New York Tower site revealed that the caisson would have

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:14.920
to descend to the appalling depth of 106 feet below water level, all other engineers shrank back

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:21.890
and I had to face this enormous and dangerous task, spending almost two years in compressed air and

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:30.000
nearly losing my life. Colonel Roebling was brought up out of the New York caisson, nearly insensible. All that

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.320
his death was hourly expected. They were suffering from what is now called the

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:42.150
bends, caisson disease, an excruciatingly painful affliction caused by nitrogen

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:46.630
bubbles in the bloodstream. By December Colonel Roebling found himself too weak

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:53.050
and ill to go down to the bridge anymore. At first I thought I would succumb, but I

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:58.870
had a tower to lean upon, my wife, a woman of infinite tact and wisest counsel.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:04.480
Washington had met his bride eight years earlier during the Civil War.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:10.740
We've just had the most successful ball ever given by any army or by anyone.

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:16.070
Miss Emily Warren, sister of the general, came specially from West Point to attend.

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:23.660
It was the first time I ever saw her, and I am very much of the opinion that she has captured my heart at last.

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:29.670
He is unpretentious in manner, and one might scan him for hours and see no traits.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.590
except his continuous quiet and strong self-composure.

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:40.470
He is a good classical scholar, a fine linguist, and as a mineralogist, he is nearly as widely

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:46.150
known as he is as an engineer.

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:51.920
Now confined to his bed, Washington continued the work with Emily's help.

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:54.470
She began by taking dictation.

00:20:54.470 --> 00:21:00.000
As time went on, as she grew fluent in engineering, she became his most valued emissary, his

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:07.110
eyes, his ears, his good right arm, a diplomat, and a soother of damaged egos. She was also the

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:16.290
one person whose company he could tolerate. From fossils brought up out of the riverbed,

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:22.140
Roebling determined that the strata upon which the caisson now rested had not shifted in several

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:29.730
million years. Faced with the inevitability of another year's digging and the loss of perhaps

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:30.000
a hundred lives,

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:35.330
risk of a weak foundation and staking his reputation on his own judgment he

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:42.100
halted the caisson's descent almost 30 feet short of bedrock the decision

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:47.680
entirely roeblings was the most daring and difficult of the whole project and to

00:21:47.680 --> 00:22:00.000
this day the New York tower does not stand on bedrock but on sand towers 1870 to 1880

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:16.500
While the newspapers and the public were transfixed by the drama inside the caissons, the two stone towers grew ever taller and more conspicuous.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:25.750
If the caissons were the test of Roebling's courage and intelligence as an engineer, the towers were the tangible proof that the bridge was going up.

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:30.000
Brooklyn Eagle, September 12th.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:54.260
28th 1872. Everything possible is done by steam. The stone is stored at Red Hook and a scow brings it to the structure every day. The stones are lifted from the scow by steam and placed on two cars on a track. Steam derricks raise them from the cars and place them in their proper position. The spaces are filled up with concrete. Even this is mixed by machinery.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:13.760
A communication from the stonecutters

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:18.090
asking for an increase of 5 cents an hour to their wage

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:21.620
was referred to a committee.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.580
The pay of the laborers is as follows.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:27.430
Masons, 30 cents an hour.

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.000
Day laborers, 12 and 1/2 cents

